The context menu Ladybird opens on a misspelled word starts with a
spelling block: up to five of the spellchecker’s guesses (or a disabled
No Guesses Found), then Ignore Spelling and Learn Spelling. That order
follows WebKit’s ContextMenuController, which lists every guess (or No
Guesses Found), then Ignore Spelling and Learn Spelling. The cap of five
is kMaxSpellingSuggestionsLimit in Chrome’s SpellingMenuObserver.

Ladybird’s pages each keep the words the user ignored or learned, and
drop them from every spellcheck answer that comes back — including
answers to checks that ran before the spellchecker knew the word. Chrome
does the same with its renderer’s copy of the user’s custom dictionary:
SpellCheck::CreateTextCheckingResults() skips any result whose word the
dictionary holds. But Chrome has no Ignore Spelling in its context menu,
and the one in the macOS Spelling panel goes straight to NSSpellChecker,
so nothing drops ignored words from answers already on their way.
